+**Self-heal recovery recipe (Phase 1e, `SELF_HEAL_*`) — distinct from the `helper-unreachable` row.** When `__wait_cluster_ready` times out (the sole `cluster-unhealthy` site — `scripts/layer7.sh::__self_heal_cluster`), the self-heal does NOT blindly recreate. It probes the REAL cause via a *positive* host-helper signal (`crates/devloop-helper/src/commands.rs::probe_apiserver_reachable`: control-plane container running + apiserver `/readyz`, bounded retry — never a parsed kubectl error string), emits `SELF_HEAL CASE=<…> ACTION=<…>` once the bounded probe returns a verdict (on every reached path, including the no-action ones), and applies at most ONE fix:
+
+| `SELF_HEAL CASE=` | `ACTION=` | Meaning / outcome |
+|---|---|---|
+| `apiserver-unreachable` | `recreate` | Control-plane container down OR apiserver positively unreachable → `dev-cluster recreate` (a host verb that INDEPENDENTLY re-confirms control-plane-dead before it destroys, captures a pre-destroy `EVIDENCE=` bundle, and destroys only this slug's cluster). Bounded once-per-session (`self-heal-recreate.attempted` marker). Re-verifies → `SELF_HEAL RESULT=recovered` (proceed) or `RESULT=failed` (→ `cluster-self-heal-failed`). |
+| `kubeconfig-stale` | `restore-kubeconfig` | Helper reports the cluster healthy but the gate couldn't see it → `dev-cluster restore-kubeconfig` (**restore ONLY — reaches no teardown/delete/create path, derives the port from the live cluster**). Re-verifies → recovered/failed. |
+| `rollout-wedged` | `none` | Apiserver answers but pods aren't settling → bare `cluster-unhealthy` (pod-logs row above). Never recreated. |
+| `probe-inconclusive` | `none` | Reachability `Unknown` / setup-in-progress / unmatched combo → `cluster-self-heal-probe-inconclusive`. Fail-closed. |
+| `helper-unreachable` | `none` | The bounded `dev-cluster status` probe itself couldn't reach the helper → `helper-unreachable` (host lane). |
+
+The terminal state after a fix is ALWAYS `SELF_HEAL RESULT=recovered` or an escalation — a bare `cluster-unhealthy` never follows `recovered`. The destroy is bounded once per container session and re-confirmed host-side, so a healthy cluster is never destroyed (a false "unreachable" fails closed to escalation, not to a destroy). **Positive control:** no `SELF_HEAL CASE=` line on a `cluster-unhealthy` log means the self-heal block never ran (a regression) — the absence is the signal. **Contrast the `helper-unreachable` row:** that lane is a dead/crashed HELPER (host-only, restart via `devloop.sh`); this recipe is for a LIVE helper against an unhealthy cluster. ADR-0030 is the host-side lifecycle contract.
+
 **Phase 1f — observability-stack readiness (per-probe; task #56 user-ruling (a)).** After pods-healthy (1e), Layer 7 waits for the observability HTTP endpoints the suite probes — the same `ENV_TEST_*_URL` the suite uses, so no gate-vs-suite drift — with **per-probe disposition** (NOT a blanket suppressor): **Prometheus HARD** (`/-/ready` → `PRECONDITION_FAILURE observability-prometheus-not-ready` if it never readies, since the metrics tests one-shot-query it) and **Loki SOFT** (`/ready` → on budget-expiry a loud greppable `WARN LOKI_NOT_READY_AFTER=<n>s` that pre-attributes the eventual `test_all_services_have_logs_in_loki` failure to the observability stack — "NOT your diff" — then PROCEEDS, honoring the crate's optional-Loki semantics). Grafana is skipped (no suite queries its HTTP API). This closes the cold-start gap: a cold-but-coming Loki becomes a Phase-1 WAIT (→ suite green), not a Phase-2 FAIL. A **genuinely-ABSENT** Loki still surfaces as a loud Phase-2 FAIL (no masking) until the deferred env-tests-crate fix (`docs/TODO.md` §Env-Test Resilience — `is_loki_available` retry / conditional-skip). The HTTP probe is a `DEVLOOP_TEST`-gated seam (`HTTP_PROBE`, fixed `curl` in production), same class as the other layer7 seams.
 
 **Phase 1h — per-run organization (R-7, story task #3).** After 1f, Layer 7 generates a subdomain (`e2e-<16 lowercase hex>`, no inputs), provisions ONE fresh organization via `infra/kind/scripts/setup.sh --provision-org <sub>` (bounded by `DEVLOOP_ORG_PROVISION_TIMEOUT`, default 120s), then verifies AC resolves it (AC `/health` bounded by `DEVLOOP_HEALTH_BUDGET`, default 300s; the org-resolution probe itself bounded by `DEVLOOP_ORG_PROBE_TIMEOUT`, default 10s), and exports the subdomain to BOTH Phase-2 suites (`ENV_TEST_ORG_SUBDOMAIN` + `E2E_ORG_SUBDOMAIN`). **Why it exists:** no production code marks a meeting ended, so an org's live-meeting count only climbs toward `max_concurrent_meetings` — the browser suite creates ~7 meetings/run against the shared `demo` org's cap of 10, so run 2 against the same cluster used to fail with a 403 the pipeline attributed to the diff. A per-run org makes run N's verdict independent of runs 1..N−1 for everything keyed by `org_id`. (NOT for anything keyed above it: AC's registration limiter counts `auth_events` by IP, org-independent — same mechanism one level up, unchanged by this step.)
